---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import { deck } from "../lib/deck";

const cards = await deck();
const repo = "rayh1/sowertarot";
---

<Base title="About" description="What the Sower Tarot deck is, how the daily card is drawn, how the cards are made, and the licence.">
  <article class="about prose">
    <h1>About</h1>

    <h2 id="deck">The deck</h2>
    <!-- The seed mark of public/favicon.svg without its card frame, in the page's colours. -->
    <svg class="seed-mark" viewBox="8 6 17 22" aria-hidden="true">
      <path class="ink" d="M16 21.5 C15.4 17.5 15.6 14.5 16.6 11" fill="none" stroke-width="2.2" stroke-linecap="round" />
      <path class="ink" d="M16.1 15.2 C13.6 15.6 10.6 14.2 9.6 10.8 C12.8 10.4 15.4 12.2 16.1 15.2 Z" stroke-width="0.4" />
      <path class="ink" d="M16.5 12.2 C17.4 9.4 20.2 7.4 23.2 7.8 C22.6 10.8 19.6 12.6 16.5 12.2 Z" stroke-width="0.4" />
      <ellipse class="seed" cx="16" cy="23.6" rx="4.6" ry="3" />
    </svg>
    <p>
      Sower Tarot is a deck that is still being found. It is named after its central card, The Sower: casting what
      you have across ground you cannot control, trusting that some of it will take. The deck grows the same way,
      and only when two of its own cards call for it. When The Sower is the morning's card, a new card grows from a
      seed that comes from outside the deck: something from the day, a random page of a book, a sentence someone
      else said. When The Miner is the morning's card, the new card is dug from inside, from a gap between two
      cards: a meaning the deck circles but no card holds. Several candidate cards sprout from each seed or gap, and
      a new card has to differ in meaning from every card already in the deck. Most candidates do not take; one is
      kept, or none, and then the search goes on until one is. There is no final size: there is always further to
      go.
    </p>
    <p>
      The cards borrow the look of the old Rider-Waite deck, but not its meanings. No card has a fixed meaning,
      upright or reversed. Meaning forms through use: each morning one card is drawn and read fresh for that day,
      and a second, hidden card, the lens card, sets the angle it is read from. The same card can say something
      different on another day.
    </p>
    <p>
      The deck is for anyone who wants a card and a question to carry through the day; no knowledge of tarot is
      needed. It does not tell fortunes and does not give advice: a reading is a question to consider, not an
      answer. Read together, the readings are also a record of a search, of where the deck has looked so far.
    </p>

    <h2 id="draw">How the daily draw works</h2>
    <p>
      The card is drawn every morning at seven (Amsterdam time).
    </p>
    <p>
      Nobody picks the card. It comes from <a href="https://drand.love">drand</a>, a public source of random
      numbers run by the League of Entropy, a group of independent organisations such as universities and internet
      companies. Every thirty seconds they publish a new number, counted by round. Each organisation holds one
      piece of a shared secret key, and a round's number can only be worked out when enough of them combine their
      pieces. So no single organisation, and nobody outside, can know a number before it is published or change it
      afterwards, and anyone can check that a published number is genuine.
    </p>
    <p>
      The draw uses the number published at seven. The cards are lined up in alphabetical order, and the number is
      divided by the number of cards: what is left over points to the card. The rest of the number, divided the
      same way, picks the lens card from the remaining cards and decides whether the card is upright or reversed.
      Each reading shows the round its card came from.
    </p>
    <p>
      Two cards do more than set the day's question. From 28 October 2026 on, when The Sower or The Miner is drawn
      as the morning's card, upright or reversed, the deck gains exactly one new card, and no card enters it any
      other way. The lens card never counts. The new card's page names the morning that called for it.
    </p>
    <p>
      Because every round's number follows from that shared key, the numbers for all mornings to come are already
      fixed. Nobody can know them until they are published, the deck's maker included. So for a given deck, every
      future morning's card is already settled but unknown. Only the deck changing keeps the sequence open: when a
      card enters or leaves, the alphabetical line-up shifts and every later morning falls differently. A new card
      is announced by the draw that calls for it, but which morning that will be cannot be foreseen.
    </p>
    <p>
      Anyone can check a draw with{" "}
      <a href={`https://github.com/${repo}/blob/main/scripts/draw_check.py`}><code>draw_check.py</code></a>, a small
      Python program in the site's public repository on GitHub, which recomputes any day's card from its round and
      the deck as it stood that morning.
    </p>

    <h2 id="made">How the cards are made</h2>
    <p>
      Every picture in this deck is AI-generated. The cards are rendered on a home computer with FLUX.1 [dev], an
      open image model, and a small add-on model trained for this deck that gives it its woodcut look: heavy ink
      outlines, hatching and flat colour.
    </p>
    <p>
      Each card starts as a written scene, usually one figure, one object and one gesture. It is rendered many
      times, each time from a different random starting point, and the maker picks one. Small faults, such as a
      stray ornament at the edge, are repaired with an AI image editor (Qwen Image). The border, the title banner and the lettering are
      not drawn by the model: a script adds them, so every card has the same frame.
    </p>
    <p>
      The moving pictures move only where something should move. For {cards.filter((c) => /_code_/.test(c.data.clip ?? "")).length} of
      the {cards.length} cards a script draws the motion (ripples, gliding birds, flickering candles, glowing
      light) over the card's own pixels, so the ink lines stay exactly as they were. For the other {cards.filter((c) => !/_code_/.test(c.data.clip ?? "")).length}, where the
      motion is hard to draw (fire, pouring water, breaking waves), an AI video model (Wan 2.2) animates a masked
      part of the card. The maker watched and chose every clip.
    </p>

    <h2 id="check">Check a card</h2>
    <p>
      Every original card image and clip on this site is listed in <a href="/checksums.txt">checksums.txt</a>{" "}
      with its SHA-256 checksum: a 64-character fingerprint of the file's exact bytes. The same list is in the{" "}
      <a href={`https://github.com/${repo}/blob/main/content/checksums.txt`}>repository</a>, whose history shows
      when each file changed. To check a file you downloaded, compute its checksum and look for it in the list:
    </p>
    <pre><code>{`sha256sum card_the-sower.png            # Linux
shasum -a 256 card_the-sower.png        # macOS
Get-FileHash card_the-sower.png         # Windows PowerShell (prints capitals)`}</code></pre>
    <p>
      Or put the list next to your downloads and run <code>sha256sum -c --ignore-missing checksums.txt</code>.
    </p>
    <p>
      A match means the file is the original, byte for byte. Each original also carries a label inside it, in
      its XMP metadata: made by a trained AI model (the IPTC digital source type
      <em>trainedAlgorithmicMedia</em>), creator Sower Tarot, licence CC BY-NC-SA 4.0. A copy that was resized,
      recompressed or stripped of that label, as most social media do, no longer matches. That shows only that
      it is not the original file, not that the picture was changed.
    </p>

    <h2 id="licence">Licence and credits</h2>
    <p>
      The cards, clips and texts are licensed under{" "}
      <a href="https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by-nc-sa/4.0/">CC BY-NC-SA 4.0</a>: you may share and adapt
      them for non-commercial use, if you credit Sower Tarot and share your version under the same licence. The
      site's code is licensed under the MIT licence.
    </p>
    <p>
      Made with FLUX.1 [dev] by Black Forest Labs, Qwen Image and Wan 2.2 by Alibaba, ComfyUI, Claude by Anthropic,
      and drand by the League of Entropy. Type: IM Fell English, EB Garamond and Cormorant Garamond.
    </p>
    <p>
      The site, its cards and texts, and the skills that make new cards and the daily reading are open source at{" "}
      <a href={`https://github.com/${repo}`}>github.com/{repo}</a>.
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
